<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # I Learned About the History of Panama – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 10px 0 0 0;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 10th Grade (CEFR: B1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Learning to Research History</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Learned About the History of Panama.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Identify a simple chronological sequence in a recorded narrative or dialogue. Interactive: Respond to and clarify multipart questions.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can identify chronological events in a historical narrative or recorded interview about Panama (e.g., "What happened after the Torrijos-Carter Treaties were signed?").
Can answer multipart questions about a podcast on key historical events with clarification as needed (e.g., "Who signed the Torrijos-Carter Treaties, and what were the outcomes?").
Can listen to a historical podcast or documentary clip and list in order key events related to Panama's history (e.g., "First, the treaty was negotiated, then Panama regained control of the canal in 1999.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a large image of the signing of the Torrijos-Carter Treaties and a current image of the Panama Canal. Students are asked: "What historical event does this image depict?" and "What do you know about the *history* of the *Panama Canal*?" Students briefly share their prior knowledge in pairs.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models sentences using target vocabulary and past simple: "The *Torrijos-Carter Treaties* were *signed* in 1977. This was a *significant* event in Panama's *history*." The teacher clearly pronounces key terms like "signed" /saɪnd/ and "historical" /hɪˈstɒrɪkəl/, focusing on correct -ed endings and syllable stress for "history".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is the signing of a treaty a current event or something that *happened* in the past?" (Expected answer: In the past.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When we talk about 'chronological sequence,' do we mean events happening in any order, or in the order they *occurred*?" (Expected answer: In the order they happened.)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 10 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Learning to Research History".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A 1.5-minute audio excerpt from a historical podcast titled "Panama's Path to Independence and the Canal". The audio provides a brief narrative of Panama's *separation* from Colombia and the early *construction* of the *canal*. The register is informative and semi-formal.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Listening: Can identify chronological events in a historical narrative or recorded interview about Panama.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (First Listening - Gist):</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students listen to the podcast excerpt once.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> "What are the two main historical *events* discussed in this podcast clip?" (Expected: Panama's *separation* from Colombia and the *beginning* of the *canal construction*).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> "The podcast mentions that the *canal* *was completed* before *Panama declared independence*." (False).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students match key years (e.g., 1903, 1914) with events (e.g., *separation*, *canal construction began*).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 10 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice identifying specific chronological details and using past simple, reported speech, and passive voice with the target vocabulary (<i>history, Panama, independence, separation, canal, significance, construction, economy, events, culture, period, identity, declared, learned, shaped, completed, began, transferred, transformed, historical, relevant, significant</i>).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ol>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Sequencing Events:</b> The teacher provides a list of 5-6 events mentioned in the podcast (e.g., "Panama *declared independence*," "The U.S. *began* *canal construction*," "Panama *separated* from Colombia"). Students listen to the excerpt again and number the events in chronological order in their notebooks. (Focus on past simple: *declared*, *began*, *separated*).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Gap-fill/Clarification:</b> Students receive a handout with sentences from the podcast containing gaps or requiring a specific detail. They listen a third time to complete the sentences. Example: "Historians say the *canal* _________ *Panama's economy* significantly." (Expected: *transformed*). After filling, students work in pairs to clarify answers, asking, "What word *was* it for the second gap?" or "Could you spell *transformed*?" (Focus on reported speech and pronunciation of /s/-/z/ and -ed endings).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction - Q&A on Chronology:</b> In pairs, students take turns asking each other multipart questions about the chronological sequence of events from the podcast, using prompt cards if needed.
                            <ul>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A: "When did *Panama separate* from Colombia, and what *happened* immediately after?"</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B: "<i>Panama separated</i> in 1903, and the *construction* of the *canal* *began* soon after. Can you tell me what the podcast *reported* about the *significance* of that *period*?" (Focus on asking for clarification and using past simple and reported speech).</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 15 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Learning to Research History".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Small Group Oral Timeline Summary. Students work in groups of three or four to create a short oral summary of the key historical *events* heard in the podcast, presented as a chronological timeline.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group discusses and agrees on the main events and their order.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They decide who will present which part of the timeline, ensuring clear communication and collaboration.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They must use at least five target vocabulary words (e.g., *independence*, *separation*, *canal*, *construction*, *transformed*) and practice asking/giving clarification, and using past simple and passive voice where appropriate.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example sentence starters: "First, *Panama declared independence*...", "Then, the *construction* of the *canal* *began*...", "Historians *reported* that this *period* *transformed* the nation..."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Groups then present their oral summary to another group or to the whole class. Peers can ask follow-up questions for clarification (e.g., "Could you clarify the *significance* of that *event*?").</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 10 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student self-reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the group presentations and discussions. The checklist will focus on individual and group contributions to chronological sequencing and use of target language.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students present their oral timeline summaries from Stage 4. The teacher circulates, listening for accurate recall and sequencing of events, appropriate use of past simple, passive voice, and target vocabulary.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of historical terms and -ed verb endings.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate use of past simple and passive voice to describe events.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clear and accurate chronological sequencing of events from the podcast.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective use of at least five target vocabulary words in context.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Active participation in group discussions and asking/responding to clarification questions.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a short exit ticket with the following prompts:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"List three key historical *events* about *Panama* that you *learned* today from the podcast."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of the podcast or activity *was* most *challenging* for you, and why?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 5 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do I feel now in identifying the chronological order of *historical events* from a listening source?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Which new vocabulary words or grammatical structures (e.g., passive voice for *events*) will be most useful for me when *researching history*?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What *worked* well in today's lesson, specifically regarding the listening comprehension tasks and vocabulary introduction? What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for students who struggled with identifying chronological sequences, especially for the pronunciation of specific sounds like /s/ and /z/, or -ed endings?</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> In Lesson 2, students will read a text about the same historical period and events discussed today. This will reinforce the vocabulary and chronological understanding established in this listening lesson and deepen their comprehension of Panama's *history* and its *significance*.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 5 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>